{"skill":{"slug":"mcporter","displayName":"Mcporter"},"version":{"version":"1.0.0","createdAt":1767545355897,"changelog":"","changelogSource":null,"license":null,"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":1480,"sha256":"475253164a0cca240679819497abd35bcd708162ab0f399990468d94d55a6d15","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":1976,"sha256":"2160633fa853a2b56e4e98551f884d820eca6e0fdd3d8109f7322ea3756843ab","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789013596050,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"f9e3e2fae2ff9bb8351d0c46647392d6f923a02b1b531ad7d630b074f5155f38","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/f9e3e2fae2ff9bb8351d0c46647392d6f923a02b1b531ad7d630b074f5155f38","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: mcporter\nVersion: 1.0.0\n\nThe skill is classified as suspicious due to its broad capabilities, particularly the explicit instruction for `mcporter call --stdio \"bun run ./server.ts\"` in `SKILL.md`. This demonstrates and enables the execution of arbitrary commands, which is a significant security risk, even though the example command itself is benign. Additionally, the skill allows for arbitrary HTTP calls (`mcporter call <url>`), configuration edits, and daemon management, granting extensive system and network access. While these capabilities might be plausible for the stated purpose of a CLI tool, they represent high-risk behaviors without clear malicious intent from the skill bundle itself.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779159615122},"skillspector":{"status":"clean","normalizedStatus":"clean","score":17,"severity":"LOW","recommendation":"SAFE","issueCount":3,"checkedAt":1789013596050},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"medium","summary":"The skill does what it says, but it gives an agent broad MCP, auth, config, local command, and daemon capabilities without enough scoping or safety guidance.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Network calls, OAuth, config mutation, local process launch, and daemon control are proportionate to a low-level MCP CLI skill, but the artifact leaves them broadly available to the agent with limited boundaries.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The skill installs the mcporter Node package by name only, with no pinned version, lockfile, integrity hash, or reviewed package provenance in the artifact.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The instructions include arbitrary remote MCP URL calls and a --stdio example that runs a local command, but they do not require confirming trusted endpoints, trusted local scripts, or user intent before high-impact calls.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The skill documents OAuth, config edits, and daemon start/stop/restart, which can persist credentials, settings, or running processes, but it only identifies the default config path and does not describe credential storage or persistence implications.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The stated purpose is to use the mcporter CLI for MCP server listing, auth, config, HTTP/stdio tool calls, daemon control, and code generation; the documented capabilities match that purpose.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"note"}],"guidance":"Install only if you want an agent to operate mcporter with broad MCP authority. Before use, confirm any OAuth login, config change, daemon start, remote MCP URL, and --stdio command, and avoid sending secrets or running scripts unless the server or local code is trusted.","findings":"[SQP-2] expected: Auth, config changes, remote calls, and stdio execution are expected mcporter capabilities, but the artifact-backed concern remains that the skill does not add explicit trust or confirmation guidance around those high-impact actions.\n[E1] expected: The remote URL example is a disclosed MCP capability and uses placeholder domains, so it is not evidence of hidden exfiltration; users should still understand arbitrary MCP endpoints may receive provided inputs.\n[T08] unexpected: The unpinned npm package is an install-chain risk rather than a required behavior of the skill itself; there is no artifact evidence of a malicious dependency, only lack of pinning/provenance controls.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789013596050}}}}}